# Target C-II — explicit global route-surplus floor from the rhombus boundary

**Date:** 2026-09-13
**Status:** proof-only Type-II composition; theorem-state authority unchanged.
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The canonical train-boundary theorem already gives an explicit lower bound on the
global route currency.  It is stronger than the earlier `0.1224` Fejer-only bound
because it uses the entire strict-rhombus boundary before any phase atomization.

Put

\[
S_{route}:={b-\operatorname{per}(F)+Q+4f\over D},
\]

where `b` is the occurrence-counted outer contact walk, `f` is the number of bounded
nonquadrilateral faces, and `Q=sum(deg G-4)` over those faces.  Then every full-core
Type-II survivor sequence satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}S_{route}
\ge c_{route}:=
4\sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2\sqrt3}}-\pi
=0.6676585738660363\ldots .}
\tag{GR.1}
\]

Equivalently, with `delta=(b-per(F))/D` and
`P_exc=(Q+4f)/D`,

\[
\boxed{\liminf(\delta+P_{exc})\ge c_{route}.}
\tag{GR.2}
\]

## 1. Finite occurrence-safe inequality

Let `R_4` be the global number of strict-rhombus bounded faces and let `B_4` be the
number of contact-edge occurrences incident to exactly one strict rhombus.  The
canonical train-boundary theorem gives

\[
B_4\ge4\sqrt{R_4}.
\tag{GR.3}
\]

Every occurrence counted by `B_4` has a non-rhombus face on its other side.  The other
side is either the unbounded face or a bounded nonquadrilateral face: every bounded
quadrilateral contact face is itself a strict rhombus.  Therefore the occurrence-safe
recipient count gives

\[
B_4\le b+\sum_{G\ {m non4}}\deg G=b+Q+4f.
\tag{GR.4}
\]

Combining (GR.3)--(GR.4) yields the exact finite inequality

\[
\boxed{b+Q+4f\ge4\sqrt{R_4}.}
\tag{GR.5}
\]

Subtracting `per(F)` is safe because the outer contact walk contains a cyclic walk
around the force hull, so `per(F)<=b` in the canonical post-ZF representative.  More
directly, no sign assumption on `b-per(F)` is needed for (GR.5); the subtraction is
only used after the Type-II perimeter limit below.

## 2. Type-II limit

The canonical full-core Type-II population theorem gives

\[
{R_4\over D^2}\longrightarrow
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\]

and the Type-II near-disk force-hull theorem gives

\[
{\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}\longrightarrow\pi.
\]

Divide (GR.5), subtract `per(F)/D`, and pass to `liminf`:

\[
\liminf {b-\operatorname{per}(F)+Q+4f\over D}
\ge4\sqrt{\Delta_2}-\pi,
\]

which is (GR.1).  The decimal is only an evaluation of the exact constant; the proof
uses the symbolic expression.

## 3. Immediate branch consequences

The floor gives a useful exact split of the surviving Type-II branch:

\[
\delta+P_{exc}\ge c_{route}-o(1).
\tag{GR.6}
\]

Thus a near-geodesic outer walk must carry at least `0.6676D` exceptional-face
perimeter, while a sublinear exceptional-face perimeter forces an outer-walk detour of
at least `0.6676D`.  The two currencies cannot both be cheap.

Using the exact outer ledger `b+Q=2(q-k)`, (GR.6) also gives

\[
{2(q-k)\over D}+{4f\over D}-\pi
\ge c_{route}-o(1),
\]

or

\[
\boxed{
{q-k\over D}+2{f\over D}
\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}-o(1).
}
\tag{GR.7}
\]

This is the train-count/Mantel endpoint in route form, not a new unconditional
`q-k` floor.  It remains valid when the exceptional perimeter is distributed over
many faces and when the outer walk has repeated bridge or articulation occurrences.

## 4. Interaction with current ledgers

The canonical augmented-cell payment and the exact Type-II loaded-contact identity
give `Q+4f <= 10(e_BB-k)` in the coarse form.  Hence (GR.6) implies the explicit
three-currency necessary condition

\[
\boxed{
{b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}
 +10{e_{BB}-k\over D}
\ge c_{route}-o(1).
}
\tag{GR.8}
\]

The sharper route identity `b+Q+4f=2T` gives the same floor as
`2T-per(F)`, while (GR.8) keeps the loaded/topological payment visible.  Neither
inequality supplies an upper bound on the same currencies.  In particular, a large
outer detour or a linear exceptional-face population remains compatible with the
current high-detour scalar region.

## 5. Scope

- `B_4`, `b`, and `Q+4f` are edge-occurrence quantities; no global/local boundary
  identification is made.
- The only face classification used is the canonical fact that bounded quadrilaterals
  are strict rhombi.
- The Type-II limits are full-core limits.  No claim is made for Type-I containers or
  for an arbitrary selected harmonic carrier.
- All source points, including contact-isolated points, remain present.
- This is a proved candidate at proof-only scope. It does not produce an exact unit
  triangle, a strict source-preserving diameter descent, or a Level-I conclusion.

**Disposition:** a valid explicit narrowing of the full-core Type-II branch; C-II and
Level I remain open.
